<img border="0" src="../Images/1_sportsbanner.gif"><P align="center">
<font size="4"><b>Out of the Park Baseball Interview Session: Markus Heinsohn</b></font><P align="center">
&nbsp;<P>Our resident baseball guru, John Rodriguez, had a chance to correspond
with Markus Heinsohn on the upcoming <a href="http://www.ootp.net/">Out of the
Park Baseball 2</a>.<P>&nbsp;<P>
<b>Could you give us a little on your background in gaming? What made you want to create a text based baseball
game?</b><P>
<b>Markus:</b> After playing arcade titles (HB 4 mainly) I found myself more simming games than handling the
joystick. It didn't take long to realize that the statistical accuracy of these games are
really bad, and so I decided to write a little simulator. It was really small and didn't do
much more than simming games, and while playing a game you had no choices, the computer did
everything. 2 years later I had some time, so I decided to start another project, called Out
Of The Park. One year later it really looked nice and I began to market it. I always loved the statistical accuracy of text games, and that was also the main goal for me
when I started developing OOTP. Things like the interface or the career mode came later, but
in my opinion stuff like that really enhances every game.<P>
<b>As a developer, what is the hardest part about creating a game? Is it stats?
Fun factor? What advice would you give to small party developers that are looking to create games like
OOTP?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>:</b> Well, the basic game engine of a baseball sim isn't too hard to
program. It just takes some time to properly adjust the factors that play a part in baseball. The career mode also took a lot
of time. I started by simply coding the game engine and the computer manager first. After a lot of testing
and adjusting I coded the interface and most game screens. Then came the career mode, drafting
etc. The only advice I can give is to be patient. I can't remember how often my PC
crashed, and once (while coding the computer manager) I almost threw it out of the window. Also play your
game as often as you can. And if you can't stop playing, you've done it right !<P><b>OOTP has received critical success from many gamers saying that it improved on Diamond Mind Baseball's statistical accuracy with OOTP's career play. What features in OOTP 2 will add to the career mode of
play?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>:</b> There will be a better minor league system, probably with generated minor league stats. This
way you can follow the progress of your youngsters and guide them through the levels. But like
in real life, if you call someone up to early and he struggles, he'll loose confidence and
get even worse. I try to make the career aspect of players improving a bit more realistic.&nbsp;<P>
<b>If there was one knock against OOTP, it was that it is a DOS based game. What kind of features are you able to put in with a
Windows version that you weren't able with a DOS version?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>:</b> First it absolutely looks better :-) . Also I was able to implement better
keyboard handling, so that everything will be possible to do just with the keyboard. This is useful while editing
your lineups etc. The interface will be similar to the one in OOTP 1, because I liked the idea
of the buttons, backgrounds etc. I didn't want to use the windows interface because in my opinion
it just doesn't feel like a computer game. The game's new resolution of 800*600 also gives more room, of course.<P>
<b>With the discontinuation of Sierra's baseball title, many gamers are looking for a title that will have online league play. Will online play be supported in OOTP
2? If so, what can we look forward to with OOTP 2's online gameplay?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>: </b>There will be online league support. For example the commish can send the league to the
team owners who will be able to play their games at home and send the results back to the
commish, who does the rest, like setting up the web site, doing trades etc. OOTP will also
feature HTML export functions for every report, like box scores, league leaders, standings etc.<P>
<b>What features of the upcoming version will convince me to play OOTP instead of the other text based
sims?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>: </b>The enhanced career play, the online league options, the record books, the financial system,
the beautiful interface, the great play-by-play, the enhanced statistical accuracy, the huge
amount of stats (more splits, for example pinch hitting, close/late, home/away etc, game by
game log, many streaks and much more) ........ and so on :-)<P>
<b>Let's talk about some specific details of OOTP 2. How do you plan on rating the
players? Will more categories factored into the ratings?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>: </b>The rating system will stay about the same, because it has worked very good in the past
and users liked it too.<P>
<b>Will play-by-play be primarily the same or enhanced?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>: </b>It will be edited and enhanced. For example when a player breaks a record it will be mentioned.
Also there will be more variations on common plays.<P>
<b>Will OOTP 2 have a pitch by pitch option where you can select every action for each pitch instead of selecting an option for the at-bat?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>: </b>No, there will be no pitch-by-pitch available, but I plan it for V3. This feature will take
really long to program, since the game engine would be completely different.<P>
<b>What kind of statistical categories will be added?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>: </b>Records, more streaks, more splits, game-by-game log (this is really cool since I can
use the info for the play-by-play, for example how a player fared against the opposing team,
how his batting average is in his current hitting streak, if the player is really hot or rather cold etc.) and some little things, like pitchers hold rating, defensive innings etc.<P>
<b>The overall AI of the original game was somewhat easy. How will the AI change in future
versions?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>: </b>I'll adjust and enhance it, for example pinch hitting will be smarter etc. But I haven't
worked on it yet, so I can't be more specific.<P>
<b>You have mentioned on your forum that OOTP 2 will have a financial model. How complex will this feature be?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>: </b>Not quite as complex as the one in Baseball Mogul. You will be able to negotiate
contracts with players and there will be a realistic free-agent system. The amount of money
you earn is determined by the size of your team's market (divided in 10 steps), the amount
of fans you draw and how successful your team is. The guy who is able to guide Florida
to the Series in 10 years will have to be pretty good ! I haven't worked on the system yet, but I have quite a few ideas and I'm sure it will<br>
really enhance the game. The financial system is just an option, of course, users
can play without it if they prefer.<P>
<b>Are there any last thoughts you would like to convey to people out there about OOTP?</b><P>
<b>Markus</b><b>: </b>I'd like to thank everybody who helped me developing the game, and of course the ones
who bought Version 1 and told me their ideas and suggestions. 
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>We'd like to thank Markus for taking the time out to answer a few questions
about the game. Be sure to stop by and take a look at his latest offerings
located at the <a href="http://www.ootp.net/">OOTP Baseball</a> site.</p>
